3. Permitted Purposes and commercial boundary

A noncommercial purpose is an activity not primarily directed toward commercial
advantage or monetary compensation. Permitted Purposes include personal study,
private noncommercial use, hobby projects, noncommercial applications, teaching,
learning, and noncommercial scientific research and reproducible experiments.
These permissions are available to individuals and organizations alike.

An organization's legal form does not by itself make an activity permitted.
In particular, being a university, nonprofit, charity, or public institution
is not a blanket exemption for commercial exploitation of the Software.
Research salaries, educational tuition, grants, and sponsorship do not, by
themselves, make teaching or research commercial. Research primarily intended
to develop a proprietary commercial product, provide commercial client services,
or support commercial operations is not covered by the free permission.

Commercial Use includes selling or licensing a product incorporating the
Software; using it to provide paid, subscription, or advertising-funded services;
offering hosted databases or APIs for commercial benefit; using it in paid client
work; and internal business use supporting commercial operations. Developing
or operating such products or services is Commercial Use even before revenue
is received or when the Software itself is not separately charged for.
A startup, free tier, or nonprofit operator receives no automatic exemption.

As an express exception, developing, testing, reviewing, documenting, and
submitting improvements for inclusion in the public NaOnDB project is permitted,
including employer-sponsored participation. This exception does not authorize
separate commercial deployment, distribution, or client work with the Software.
Submission terms for contributions accepted upstream are separate from this
License; ordinary use and private forks do not require a contributor agreement.

